{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Issachar is a strong-boned donkey, crouching between the saddlebags.</b> Just as this donkey — it and its load are bound together and never separate, so the acquisition of Torah is bound fast and never separates from him — unlike the acquisitions of Zebulun, for since he and his house and his acquisitions are separate, he is therefore always in motion — either toward his house or toward his acquisitions, for the most part they are outside of him. But Issachar — his load is always the burden of the word of YHWH, and he crouches between the saddlebags, that is, between two boundaries. For all the successes of the body surround him, as it is written (Proverbs 3:16): ״אֹרֶךְ יָמִים בִּימִינָהּ, בִּשְׂמֹאולָהּ עֹשֶׁר וְכָבוֹד״. And in every place he walks or stands, he is always between two boundaries, for they grow with him and go at his feet. This is not so with one who walks simply in commerce, for these two boundaries are not bound to him: sometimes he walks in a place of danger, which opposes the lengthening of his days and weakens his strength on the road, and sometimes he is distanced from his wealth, for his honor will not descend after it — and if so, for the most part he is not between the saddlebags. But one who engages in Torah and crouches beneath the burden of the word of YHWH is always between the saddlebags, as stated." "<b>And he saw rest, that it was good.</b> For the acquisition of Torah does not depend on movement like the acquisition of money, for the most part from afar he brings his bread (Proverbs 31:14), but the acquisition of Torah depends on the labor of the soul and the rest of the body, for on this account the Torah was given on the Sabbath, a day of rest. <b>And the land, that it was pleasant</b> — for the land of Israel, its air makes wise (Baba Batra 158b). <b>And he bent his shoulder to bear</b> the yoke of Torah. And even though the acquisition of Torah precedes in rank the acquisition of mere commerce, for טוֹב סַחְרָהּ מִכָּל סְחוֹרָה (Proverbs 3:14), nevertheless he placed Zebulun before Issachar, for if there is no flour there is no Torah (Avot 3:17), and if he does not eat and drink, how will his limbs be strong and healthy to toil in the Torah of YHWH? Therefore he placed Zebulun first, who from afar brings his bread, so that Issachar would be able to sit in tents. And Issachar too gives a share of his Torah to Zebulun, who renders him tribute, עוֹבֵד עֲבוֹדָה מַמָּשׁ, serving actual service — and this is what it says: <b>and he became a slave at forced labor</b>."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:30.530073430Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}